compiler.md:36:## What the compiled path cannot compile yet
compiler.md:38:For a construct that it cannot compile yet, the code generator calls `sayWhat` or throws another `CompilerError`. Such a call or throw is a code-generator wall. The code generator has no case for these constructs:
compiler.md:64:- a local function with the `atomic` modifier: 'Can't compile a local function with this modifier yet'.
compiler.md:66:A type assumption `e asif T` has the value of `e`, but the call around it chooses its declaration as if `e` were a `T`, a supertype of `e`'s type (`Specification/basic/expressions/type-annotation.tex`). The chosen declaration is the one that the checker chooses at the assumed types. A dotted call's receiver is not one of its arguments. The code generator compiles an assumption in these places, and elsewhere as the value of `e`:
compiler.md:68:- at a dotted call's receiver, `(r asif T).m(a)`, where every argument is an assumption or `T` has one method `m`;
compiler.md:69:- at a call of a functional method whose every argument is an assumption;
compiler.md:70:- at a call of an overloaded function or operator that the call's own component declares. Where only some arguments are assumptions, the call runs the chosen declaration or a more specific one that takes the assumed types. It chooses by the other arguments' run-time types, as walk does.
compiler.md:72:It stops with 'Can't compile a dispatched call whose arguments are not all type assumptions' in these forms (ledger row 818):
compiler.md:74:- a call of a functional method with an assumption at some of its arguments but not all;
compiler.md:75:- `(r asif T).m(a)`, where `T` overloads `m` and an argument is not an assumption;
compiler.md:76:- `r.m(a asif U)`, where `r` is not an assumption and `r`'s trait overloads `m`;
compiler.md:77:- a call of an overloaded function with an assumption at some arguments but not all, where the call can run a functional method of the same name, but not every declaration more specific than the chosen one. An example is `pick(n, b asif Named)` beside an object's `pick(self, y: Named)`.
compiler.md:81:- a call of an overloaded function with an assumption at some arguments but not all, where a declaration more specific than the chosen one is generic: 'Can't compile a type assumption at some arguments but not all of a call that a generic declaration can take yet';
compiler.md:82:- a call of an overloaded function of another component with an assumption at an argument: 'Can't compile a type assumption at a call of an overloaded function of another component yet';
compiler.md:83:- a generic method on an assumed receiver: 'Can't compile a type assumption at the receiver of a generic method yet';
compiler.md:84:- a call of an overloaded function whose chosen declaration is generic, with an assumption at an argument: 'Can't compile a type assumption at a call of an overloaded generic function yet';
compiler.md:85:- a call, at an assumption of a generic trait, of a method whose parameter has the trait's type parameter, as `stamp(Q asif Tagged[\String\], s asif String)` for `stamp(self, x: T)` in `Tagged[\T\]`: 'Can't find the body that Tagged[\String\] gives stamp at ...' (ledger row 1088).
compiler.md:87:At an assumption of a trait, the call runs the trait's body, also where the object overrides it.
compiler.md:93:Until the switch-over, a compiled test uses none of these. If a test needs one, write it as an `XXX` test that names the wall (`tests-writing.md`).
compiler.md:108:- The compiled code keeps each mutable variable in a cell (`compiler/runtimeValues/MutableFValue.java`), and an object's `var` field in a cell that the object holds. Before each read and write of the cell, the generated code asks `BaseTask.inATransaction()` (`runtimeSystem/`). Inside an `atomic` block, the access goes through the transaction. So the transaction tracks the variables that the block touches. From the switch-over, it also tracks each element of the one library's arrays, by array and index: `compiler/runtimeValues/FortressArrayStore.java` calls the transaction's methods `TXReadSlot` and `TXWriteSlot`. Walk does not run this class, so test an edit of it with `ArrayStoreJUTest`, beside it.
compiler.md:123:To test a helper on the compiled path, write a compiled test whose own declarations bind it, as `compiler_tests/NativeHelperBindingCompiled.fss` does. To test a binding of the one library, put the test in `one_library_tests/`, whose tests compile against the one library and run against its jars (`tests-running.md`, "Compiled tests"). Such a test can use none of the constructs that "What the compiled path cannot compile yet" lists.
compiler.md:146:- An `atomic` block (`atomic do ... end`) or atomic expression (`atomic e`, as `atomic 1`), a `try` or a `label` block as a call's argument after the first, as `f(k, atomic do ... end)`, fails with a `VerifyError` at class load, unless the arguments fork (ledger row 322). So does a `try` as an operator's operand after the first (ledger row 526). Bind its value in a statement of its own.
compiler.md:155:  - At a place that fixes a type (`revival-changes.md`, "Numbers"), the checker refuses an atomic expression whose value is a numeral, as `atomic if c then 1 else 0 end`, and `atomic 1` fails the JVM's verification (ledger row 1043). Write `atomic do ... end`.
compiler.md:156:- An `exit` that runs after its `label` block has completed, from a function made in the block, ends a compiled program with the Java exception `LabelExit`, which no `catch` clause takes. Under walk, a `catch` clause of the library's `LabelException` takes it. The rules do not settle which is right (ledger row 1041).
compiler.md:167:- Until the switch-over, the prelude's `String` gives `<`, `=`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` and `=/=` the placeholder body `false`. A string value is a `JavaString`, whose own declarations compare. A call at an assumption runs the trait's body ("What the compiled path cannot compile yet"), so `("ab" asif String) < ("cd" asif String)` is `false` (ledger row 1089). Compare strings without an assumption.
compiler.md:242:- If your change is near transactions, top-level mutable state or the class loader's first load, do the four-thread atomic runs (`gate.md`).
tests-writing.md:52:- `one_library_tests/`: a test whose program compiles against the one library: a test of a binding of the one library, or of a defect that only that library shows. Its tests compile with `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one`, and `ant testOneLibrary` and `testFast` run them. A plain run test there runs against the one library's jars, as `OneLibraryRunHello.test` does. `compiler.md`, "What the compiled path cannot compile yet", lists what it cannot run, and `tests-running.md`, "Compiled tests", says how to run it.
tests-writing.md:108:- For a code-generator wall (`compiler.md`, "What the compiled path cannot compile yet"), write `XXXName.test` with `tests=XXXName`, `compile` and `compile_exception_contains=<text of the exception>`.
tests-writing.md:141:- If your change touches mutable state, a field, an `atomic` block, library code that writes shared state, or the Java of tasks, transactions or the class loader, run its checks at one thread and at four. `harness-one.sh`, `junit.sh` and the suites force four threads. For one thread, run the program directly with the prefix `FORTRESS_THREADS=1` (a compiled program: `compiler.md`, "Running"), and check its exit code and output.
